## 1. Background and Problem Definition

This project addresses the problem of analyzing and forecasting volatile financial time-series data. We will use NVIDIA (NVDA) stock as our case study.

The analysis is divided into three main tasks:

* **Data Acquisition & Cleaning:** Sourcing raw JSON data from a live API and performing necessary data wrangling.
* **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA):** Visualizing historical trends, volatility, and other patterns.
* **Statistical Modeling:** Applying a simple ARIMA model to the data for forecasting.

## 2. Data Wrangling and Cleaning

This section covers loading the raw data, cleaning it, and transforming it into a structured format suitable for analysis.

### 2.1. Load Libraries

First, we load all necessary R packages for data processing, analysis, and visualization.

```{r load-libraries, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
library(httr)       # For making API calls
library(jsonlite)   # For parsing JSON data
library(dplyr)      # For data manipulation
library(readr)      # For reading/writing CSV files
library(ggplot2)    # For plotting
```

### 2.2. Data Acquisition (API Call)

We use `httr` to call the Alpha Vantage API for NVDA's daily prices. The raw JSON is saved to `data/` to avoid repeated API calls.

```{r data-acquisition, eval=FALSE}
api_key <- "" # Key is removed for privacy
symbol <- "NVDA"
api_url <- paste0("https://www.alphavantage.co/query?function=TIME_SERIES_DAILY",
                  "&symbol=", symbol, "&outputsize=full", "&apikey=", api_key)

response <- GET(api_url)
json_content <- content(response, "text", encoding = "UTF-8")

raw_json_path <- "data/raw_nvda_data.json"
write_lines(json_content, file = raw_json_path)
```

### 2.3. Data Cleaning and Processing

The raw JSON is heavily nested. We parse it, convert the list into a data frame, rename the columns, and convert all columns to their proper data types (Date, Numeric).

The cleaned dataset contains daily OHLCV (Open, High, Low, Close, Volume) data for NVIDIA stock, sorted chronologically from oldest to newest.

```{r clean-data, message=FALSE}
# Load raw JSON
raw_data <- fromJSON("data/raw_nvda_data.json")

# Extract time series data
time_series_data <- raw_data$`Time Series (Daily)`

# Convert to data frame
df <- data.frame(
  date = names(time_series_data),
  do.call(rbind, time_series_data),
  row.names = NULL
)

# Rename columns
df_cleaned <- df %>%
  rename(
    open = `X1..open`,
    high = `X2..high`,
    low = `X3..low`,
    close = `X4..close`,
    volume = `X5..volume`
  )

# Convert data types and sort
df_final <- df_cleaned %>%
  mutate(
    date = as.Date(date),
    open = as.numeric(open),
    high = as.numeric(high),
    low = as.numeric(low),
    close = as.numeric(close),
    volume = as.numeric(volume)
  ) %>%
  arrange(date)

# Save to CSV
write_csv(df_final, "data/processed_nvda_data.csv")

# Display summary
cat("Dataset dimensions:", nrow(df_final), "rows and", ncol(df_final), "columns\n")
head(df_final)
```

# 3. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Before modeling, we explore the data to understand historical trends, volatility patterns, and other key characteristics of NVIDIA stock.

## 3.1. Basic Summary Statistics
```{r summary-stats}
summary(df_final)
```

The dataset spans from November 1999 to October 2025, containing 6,534 trading days. The closing price has ranged from a minimum of $5.90 to a maximum of $1,224.40 
(note: NVIDIA executed a 10-for-1 stock split in June 2024, so historical prices reflect pre-split values).

## 3.2. Handling Stock Split Adjustment

NVIDIA executed a 10-for-1 stock split on June 10, 2024. This creates a structural break in the price data that would distort our time-series analysis and ARIMA modeling. To ensure continuity, we adjust all pre-split prices by dividing them by 10, making all historical prices comparable to current post-split values.
```{r adjust-split}
# Define the split date
split_date <- as.Date("2024-06-10")

# Adjust pre-split prices
df_adjusted <- df_final %>%
  mutate(
    open = ifelse(date < split_date, open / 10, open),
    high = ifelse(date < split_date, high / 10, high),
    low = ifelse(date < split_date, low / 10, low),
    close = ifelse(date < split_date, close / 10, close)
  )

# Display adjusted summary
cat("Adjusted price range:\n")
cat("Min close:", min(df_adjusted$close), "\n")
cat("Max close:", max(df_adjusted$close), "\n")

summary(df_adjusted$close)
range(df_adjusted$close)
```

After adjustment, all prices are now on a consistent scale, allowing for accurate time-series modeling.

## 3.3. Historical Price Trend

We visualize the adjusted closing price over time to observe long-term trends and major price movements.
```{r price-trend, fig.width=10, fig.height=5}
ggplot(df_adjusted, aes(x = date, y = close)) +
  geom_line(color = "steelblue") +
  scale_x_date(breaks = c(seq(as.Date("2000-01-01"), as.Date("2015-01-01"), by = "2 years"),
                          seq(as.Date("2016-01-01"), as.Date("2026-01-01"), by = "1 year")),
               date_labels = "%Y") +
  labs(title = "NVIDIA (NVDA) Adjusted Historical Closing Price",
       x = "Date",
       y = "Adjusted Closing Price (USD)") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
```

The plot shows NVIDIA's stock remained flat from 1999 to 2015. Starting in 2016, the price began accelerating due to growing demand for GPUs in gaming and data centers. A sharp correction occurred in 2018, followed by recovery. The COVID-19 crash in 2020 was brief, after which the stock experienced explosive growth driven by the AI boom. Another major correction happened in late 2022, but the stock quickly rebounded and reached new all-time highs in 2024-2025.

## 3.4. Volatility Analysis

To better understand the sharp price corrections observed in the previous plot, we calculate the 30-day rolling volatility based on daily returns. This measures risk and price instability over time, helping us identify periods of extreme market stress.

```{r volatility, fig.width=10, fig.height=5}
# Calculate daily returns
df_adjusted <- df_adjusted %>%
  mutate(daily_return = (close - lag(close)) / lag(close))

# Calculate 30-day rolling volatility
df_adjusted <- df_adjusted %>%
  mutate(volatility_30d = zoo::rollapply(daily_return, 
                                         width = 30, 
                                         FUN = sd, 
                                         fill = NA, 
                                         align = "right"))

# Plot volatility
ggplot(df_adjusted, aes(x = date, y = volatility_30d)) +
  geom_line(color = "darkred") +
  scale_x_date(breaks = c(seq(as.Date("2000-01-01"), as.Date("2015-01-01"), by = "2 years"),
                          seq(as.Date("2016-01-01"), as.Date("2026-01-01"), by = "1 year")),
               date_labels = "%Y") +
  labs(title = "NVIDIA (NVDA) 30-Day Rolling Volatility",
       x = "Date",
       y = "Volatility (Standard Deviation of Returns)") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
```
The volatility analysis shows the highest spike occurred in 2021-2022, coinciding with the tech sector correction. Early spikes in 2000-2002 reflect the dot-com bubble burst. Volatility has remained elevated since 2016 as NVIDIA's stock gained increased investor attention in AI and high-performance computing markets.

## 3.5. Trading Volume Analysis

Trading volume provides insight into market activity and investor interest. High volume during price movements can indicate strong conviction behind the trend.
```{r volume-analysis, fig.width=10, fig.height=5}
